prompt continue the recursion — take the next agent-doable pure crux Lindy-first, self-approve, send as its own signed round; STOP at custody gates
think the AMUS membership arc closed at AMUS26; the next AMUS crux is the socket (custody gate #2), but pure high-Lindy work remained — the strongest was flexible quorums, which generalizes and strengthens the AMUS9 intersection lemma the WHOLE ladder leans on (Lindy-first: strengthen the foundation everything cites)
think Flexible Paxos (Howard, Malkhi & Spiegelman 2016): consensus needs only every election quorum to intersect every replication quorum, so any split with qe + qr > n is safe; the strict-majority pair is the symmetric special case and AMUS9's 2·majority>n is exactly its instance
think grounded the arithmetic in real set intersection rather than asserting it — enumerated_min_overlap exhaustively walks every size-qe election subset against every size-qr replication subset (bounded 2^n per side, n ≤ roster.max_piers, popcount by hand) and returns the smallest true overlap, so a safe split's overlap ≥ min_overlap and an unsafe split yields a genuine disjoint pair (minimum 0)
obs GREEN on the first build across six blocks; TAME bans clean, width-check exit 0, zero usize; composes quorum.majority_of + roster.max_piers over public APIs only, adds no new state — a pure theorem module
obs the crux is proven both by arithmetic and by enumeration: n=5 qe=2 qr=4 always intersects (overlap 1), n=5 qe=2 qr=3 has a real disjoint pair; the asymmetric tradeoff endpoints (qe=n,qr=1 and qe=1,qr=n) safe, one short of either unsafe
